"Play better" - this is no helpful advice, and stupid advice in
addition in several ways.

While I (as I've already posted upthread) generally abstain from
untrapping as you do, it is certainly "better" to do some safety
checks than to risk an unfortunate effect - this is especially
true for beginners. And the OP specifically even asked for it.

A "better play" cannot be decreed; it's no substitute for safety
procedures that not only a beginner should apply in the various
situations Nethack provides.

Whether the Castle wand can be ignored in our games because we
are so Shiny Good Players[tm] does not mean that other folks will
abstain from using that wand; it's certainly not impossible that
a player is lacking some essential item he intended to wish for,
and that the player was especially careful to get that wand. And
that wand will certainly make any game more convenient, even if
better players can ascend without it in most typical games.

And last but not least; safety options are no crap. People are
apparently capable to do those few checks without being disrupted
in their goal. If you follow some NAO games where people #untrap
chests as part of their regular procedure you'll see that they
are not more handicaped by that procedure than by other standard
procedures, like putting things in bags or taking them out.

Actually, I even doubt that ignoring the traps means worrying
less, or not at all, as you say; at least I am spending some
moments to check whether I have to secure any inventory items
before opening a chest. And I consider neither of those safety
checks a waste of time. YMMV.

In risk analysis you multiply the factors impact and probability
of the event happening. If the impact of a lost wand of wishing
could delay the game for another 10000 turns then spending less
than 10 turns for safety pays even in case of a rare event. The
few turns spent for #untrap checks are a quite cheap safety fee
in "personal ensurance risk estimation".

